main: fall back to the built-in bootcmd when the environment has none

If a tool in Linux writes a fresh environment that lacks bootcmd (fw_setenv
on a corrupt or empty area creates one from scratch), U-Boot would sit at the
prompt and the printer would not boot. Use CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND in that case;
the K1 boot path reads its parameters with defaults, so it works without any
saved variable.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [K1 OS board support on a vendor tree]

--- a/common/main.c
+++ b/common/main.c
@@ -387,6 +387,13 @@
 	else
 #endif /* CONFIG_BOOTCOUNT_LIMIT */
 		s = getenv ("bootcmd");
+#ifdef CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND
+	/* A saved environment without bootcmd (for example written by a
+	 * userspace tool from scratch) must not leave the board at the
+	 * prompt: fall back to the built-in boot command. */
+	if (!s)
+		s = CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND;
+#endif
 #ifdef CONFIG_OF_CONTROL
 	/* Allow the fdt to override the boot command */
 	env = fdtdec_get_config_string(gd->fdt_blob, "bootcmd");
